    "note": "House Draft 1 (HD1); the House passed it on third reading 2025-03-04 (43-5), and amendments made after the version read are not in the text read. The main storage duty in 134-10.5(a) is heavily bracketed so only its clean paragraph (2) is quoted. The seller-notice violation is deemed an unfair or deceptive practice (a penalty for the bill's own gun rule)."
  },
  "operative_text": "SECTION 2 (new): (a) A person engaged in the retail sale or transfer of firearms shall, at the time of sale or transfer, provide to the purchaser of a firearm a written copy of section 134-10.5.\nSECTION 2 (new): (b) Any person who violates this section shall be deemed to have engaged in an unfair or deceptive act or practice within the meaning of section 480-2.\nSECTION 3 (HRS 134-10.5): (2) Stores the firearm in a location that a reasonable person would believe to be secure; or\nSECTION 3 (HRS 134-10.5): (b) Any person who violates this section shall be guilty of a violation and fined no more than $500; provided that a person who commits the offense of criminally negligent storage of a firearm under section 707-714.5 shall be guilty of a misdemeanor.\nSECTION 3 (HRS 134-10.5): (c) Any person who violates this section shall be strictly liable for damages incurred if the firearm obtained as a result of the violation is used to injure a person or property within two years of the violation; provided that this subsection shall not apply if:\nSECTION 3 (HRS 134-10.5): (1) The injury results from a lawful act of self-defense or defense of another person; or\nSECTION 3 (HRS 134-10.5): (2) The unsecured firearm was obtained by a person as a result of a person entering or remaining on the premises unlawfully.\nSECTION 3 (HRS 134-10.5): (d) This section shall not apply to a person in an exempt category identified in section 134-11(a).\nSECTION 3 (HRS 134-10.5): (e) For purposes of this section, \"premises\" does not include a vehicle.\nSECTION 4 (HRS 707-714.5): (b) The person knows or reasonably should know that a minor is likely to gain access to the firearm without the permission of the parent or guardian of the minor; and"
}